========================================================================
0.15.0-beta  (2026-10-04)
========================================================================

WHAT THIS RELEASE IS
  The last beta before v1.0.0. It adds AX.25 packet through Direwolf and
  UZ7HO SoundModem / QtSoundModem, changes how QtC asks a BBS for mail so
  a long absence never floods the air, and clears out a list of rough
  edges found in live use on HF, VHF and Telnet. If nothing critical
  comes back from beta testers, this same code becomes v1.0.0 with a
  version bump and nothing else.

NEW: AX.25 PACKET — DIREWOLF AND SOUNDMODEM
  * Two new transports in the BBS entry dialog and the toolbar Mode
    list: Direwolf and (Qt)SoundModem. QtC talks to the modem's AGW
    port, and the modem keys the radio. Tested at 300 baud HF and 1200
    baud VHF, on Linux and Windows, including SoundModem running on a
    separate Windows PC.

  * A packet entry has Host, AGW Port and Channel. Host is filled in
    with 127.0.0.1 for a modem on the same computer. Channel uses the
    modem's own word for it — Direwolf's CHANNEL, SoundModem's modem
    A / B — and stays at 0 for one radio.

  * Everything works over packet: mail, bulletins, sending, Mail-Call,
    the Terminal view and YAPP file downloads.

  * A message is handed to the modem as one block, so it goes out in
    full frames — a long message takes far fewer frames and much less
    airtime than sending it line by line. QtC waits for the modem to
    finish transmitting before it starts timing the BBS's answer.

  * When the link drops — the modem runs out of retries, the BBS hangs
    up, or the modem is closed — the session ends at once with a plain
    message, instead of sitting out a long timeout.

  * Settings warns you when Mail-Call is pointed at a modem that is not
    answering. Nothing is transmitted to check.

HOW QtC ASKS THE BBS FOR MAIL
  * The old "list everything since last time" sweep is gone. After a
    couple of weeks away from a busy node it put hundreds of header
    lines on the air before a single byte of your own mail moved. Every
    listing is now bounded by something you control: LM for your own
    mailbox, LC for the category list (Home BBS, every 30 days), and one
    page of L> CATEGORY for each bulletin category you subscribe to.

  * QtC sets the BBS page length (OP — Settings -> App, 20 by default)
    so long output pauses instead of streaming, and answers the pauses
    itself: it keeps going inside a message you asked for, and closes a
    bulletin listing once it has what it needs. Unattended Mail-Call
    reads straight from the page menu, saving a round trip per category.

  * OP is only treated as set when the BBS actually confirms it. A
    refused value used to be recorded as confirmed and not retried for
    30 days. Settings no longer offers a page limit the BBS will refuse:
    1 to 9 snaps up to 10, LinBPQ's floor. 0 still means no paging.

  * Outgoing message lines are wrapped at 72 columns. Words are never
    split, and lines that are already short — tables, ASCII art, quoted
    text — are left exactly as typed.

HALF-DUPLEX RF: NEVER TALK OVER THE BBS
  * Every wait for a BBS reply now times out on silence, not total
    time: while text is still arriving, QtC keeps listening. A slow page
    at 300 baud used to run past the old limit, QtC moved on, and the
    BBS was left stuck at a page prompt.

  * QtC no longer mistakes a node's welcome text for the BBS prompt,
    which had put every later command one reply out of step. Page
    prompts that arrive split across several frames are read whole.

  * A BBS left holding a listing by an earlier session that dropped is
    released with a single A at login, on RF and on Telnet.

  * QtC never sends its sign-off into a page prompt, which used to leave
    the BBS session open after QtC had gone.

VIEWS, SEND / RECEIVE AND THE TERMINAL
  * Connecting from the Terminal or Debug view is now a plain terminal:
    QtC brings up the link (and answers a Telnet login) and then sends
    nothing you did not type. Connecting from Mail view, or a Mail-Call,
    runs the full session. The view you are in when you press Connect
    decides; switching views afterwards changes nothing.

  * "Send Outbox Now" is now Send / Receive, available for the whole
    session on every transport: it sends anything queued, then checks
    and downloads your personal mail. Switching to Mail view with mail
    queued no longer transmits on its own.

  * One hang-up rule: a session QtC drove hangs up when its work is
    done, on every transport. A Terminal session is yours to end.

  * The Terminal and Debug toggle buttons are now one View dropdown,
    which also gives the toolbar the Mail entry it never had. F2 / F3 /
    F4 and the View menu work as before.

  * Enter on an empty line sends a bare carriage return, so a listing
    stopped at the page limit can be continued from the Terminal. New
    CR and A quick buttons — the only two answers a page prompt takes.
    The Read button explains that it takes a file name from the Files
    area.

  * The quick buttons no longer keep keyboard focus. After clicking one,
    pressing Enter could send that command again instead of what was in
    the input box.

  * KM asks before it is sent, defaults to Cancel, and the button is
    shown in red. KM deletes the messages you have already read from the
    BBS (your copies stay in the QtC Inbox); it helps keep later
    connections short.

ABORT
  * The Stop button is now Abort, after BPQ's own A command. It stops a
    mail or bulletin download that QtC is running and returns the BBS to
    its command prompt; you stay connected.

  * It is greyed out during a YAPP file download, which cannot be
    stopped, and in the Terminal view, where the A quick button does the
    same job.

YAPP FILE DOWNLOADS
  * A completed transfer no longer hangs the session. QtC used to
    acknowledge every data block, which the YAPP protocol never asks
    for; on a slow link the extra acknowledgements backed up and the BBS
    kept answering them after the file was done. Transfers now close the
    way the protocol describes, and the session is ready again as soon
    as the file is saved.

  * A file download cannot be stopped once it starts — the BBS has
    already committed the whole file to the link. Over RF, QtC says so
    and asks before the radio keys. Telnet is instant and does not ask.

  * When the far end cancels a transfer, QtC answers the way the YAPP
    protocol requires instead of dropping it.

VARA
  * When a VARA link drops in the middle of a session — including while
    logging in — QtC now notices at once and says so. It used to wait
    out a timeout, and could report "Login failed" late, on top of the
    next session.

  * If VARA is started after QtC, QtC now links to it quietly in the
    background, so VARA's TUNE button works without restarting QtC.

MAIL-CALL
  * Always runs the full session, whichever view is open, and hangs up
    when it is done, on every transport.

  * A Mail-Call session that loses its link leaves a Notifications entry
    instead of a popup nobody is there to close.

  * After a slot that used up all its retries, the next connect you make
    yourself is treated as yours — it no longer runs as a Mail-Call.

  * The RF responsibility notice now mentions 97.221. Unattended
    automatic operation is limited to certain segments, QtC cannot see
    your dial frequency, and checking it is the operator's job.

MAIL
  * The "Body: nn" line LinBPQ puts at the top of every message is no
    longer shown — it is the BBS's stored byte count, not part of the
    message. The R: routing lines are kept.

  * Both progress counters were one behind. The number now names the
    message on the air right now, and the bar shows the work finished
    behind it. Each step is also written to the Debug view, so a saved
    log shows which message was in flight.

  * The Outbox shows each message's size in bytes, in place of a Status
    column that could only ever say "Pending".

  * Dismissing the first-visit "All or New only?" prompt with Escape or
    the window X no longer crashes QtC; it means "new only".

  * A message that failed to send no longer stalls the rest of the
    batch.

WINDOWS AND SETTINGS
  * Opening Settings no longer keys the radio for a moment on Windows.
    The PTT tab built its port list by opening every COM port in turn,
    and opening a port raises the PTT line on a Digirig. The PTT port
    for a session is now opened with its lines already set low, too.

  * Closing QtC while connected hangs up properly before it exits, so
    the BBS is not left repeating its disconnect on the air. A second
    close quits at once.

  * Three settings that never did anything are gone: "Send immediately
    on next connection to any BBS" (Compose), "Auto-download new
    personal mail on connect" and "Max message size (KB)" (Settings ->
    App). Each was saved and read back into its own box and consulted
    nowhere else. Old config.json files still load; the keys are
    ignored. The Address Book's "Send Mode" column went with the first.

HOUSEKEEPING
  * The version history moved out of both READMEs into this file.

  * A sweep removed controls and code that nothing reached, including a
    hidden Refresh button that had quietly become the app's "am I logged
    in?" flag. That is now a plain flag, and Send / Receive is the one
    button for "get my mail".

